(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-phases-of-sleep-vignettes"]={"title":"Phases of Sleep Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 68-year-old man on a medical ward is found late at night lying still with his eyes closed, not answering when the nurse enters the room. When she calls his name loudly and squeezes his shoulder, he opens his eyes and answers her questions. Which state best explains his earlier unresponsiveness?","choices":["Sleep","Coma","Quiet wakefulness","Alert wakefulness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He was unconscious, yet a loud voice and a squeeze of the shoulder roused him and he answered questions. Sleep is unconsciousness from which a person can be aroused by sensory or other stimuli, which is exactly what happened at his bedside. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 27, 28"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man's overnight recording shows a stretch of irregular, high-frequency, low-voltage waves, and the reviewer cannot tell from the electroencephalogram alone whether he is awake and active or in rapid eye movement sleep. Which additional finding best settles the question?","choices":["Heart rate showing slowing","A search for delta waves","Counting waves per second","Muscle leads showing atonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep is often difficult to tell apart from being awake and active on the electroencephalogram, because both show irregular, high-frequency waves. The difference is in the muscles: rapid eye movement sleep pairs the waking-like tracing with atonia of the voluntary muscles, sparing the eyes and diaphragm. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 29, 28, 10, 14"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man's 8-hour sleep recording is scored stage by stage. Which stage takes up the largest share of his total sleep time?","choices":["Stage N2","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N3","Stage N1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage N2 makes up the majority of the sleep cycle and is the longest stage by total duration. Rapid eye movement sleep fills about 25% of a young adult's sleep, and stage N2 outlasts both the brief drowsy stage N1 and the deep stage N3, so it holds the largest share of his night. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 13, 28, 9"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman notices that her day moves between focused work, a moment of fear, an evening of exhilaration and then sleep. She asks what generates these different states. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Outside stimuli acting on a passive brain","Fatigued muscles signaling the brain","Sleep centers that stay switched on","Activating or inhibiting brain forces"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The brain has many states of activity, including sleep, wakefulness, extreme excitement and different levels of mood such as exhilaration, depression and fear, and they arise, for the most part, from different activating or inhibiting forces generated within the brain. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 18, 19"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman's sleep recording shows rapid eye movements, irregular breathing and an electroencephalogram resembling wakefulness. How is her overall brain metabolism expected to change during this period?","choices":["Falls along with muscle tone","Rises 2 to 4 times over","Rises by up to 20%","Falls by 10 to 30%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep is an active form of sleep in which the brain is very active, and overall brain metabolism can increase by as much as 20% because of that increased brain activity. Her rapid eye movements, irregular breathing and waking-like tracing place her in this stage. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 12, 26"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman's overnight recording is scored stage by stage. About what share of her total sleep time is rapid eye movement sleep expected to fill?","choices":["About 5%","About 25%","About 50%","About 75%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep occurs in episodes and occupies about 25% of a young adult's sleep time, and at 24 she is a young adult. The rest of her night is non-rapid eye movement sleep, of which stage N2 is the largest part. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 28"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman asks whether the cause of the daily cycle between sleep and wakefulness is fully understood. Which reply is accurate?","choices":["No; there are proposed theories","Yes; the sleep centers alone control it","No; the brain plays no part in it","Yes; it simply follows muscle fatigue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. There is no definitive explanation of the sleep-wakefulness cycle, but there are proposed theories: release of the reticular activating nuclei from inhibition starts wakefulness, positive feedback sustains it, and fatigue of the activating system ends it. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 19, 3"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman's partner notices that late in the night her eyes dart rapidly under closed lids, her breathing becomes irregular and her fingers twitch. When woken, she describes a vivid dream. Which stage was she in?","choices":["Rapid eye movement sleep","Non-rapid eye movement stage N3","Non-rapid eye movement stage N2","Quiet, relaxed wakefulness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep is the active form of sleep associated with vivid dreaming, rapid eye movements, irregular muscle movements, and irregular heart rate and breathing. Late-night timing fits too, since rapid eye movement periods lengthen in the later cycles. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 13, 14, 7, 28"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man's sleep study shows a mixed, low-voltage electroencephalogram with sawtooth waves, rapid eye movements and atonia of his limb muscles. What explains the atonia?","choices":["Increased parasympathetic tone","Fatigued activating-system neurons","Exhausted muscle energy stores","Inhibited spinal motor neurons"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His tracing, rapid eye movements and atonia place him in rapid eye movement sleep. Muscle tone is very depressed because spinal muscle control areas are inhibited; the inactivity is due to inhibited alpha motor neurons, sparing the eye muscles and the diaphragm. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 28, 14, 19"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man's recording, about 90 minutes after he falls asleep, shows irregular, high-frequency waves like those of an awake, active person, while he lies asleep with atonic limbs. Which term names this stage?","choices":["Light stage N1 sleep","Paradoxical sleep","Slow-wave sleep","Quiet wakefulness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In rapid eye movement sleep the electroencephalogram shows brain wave patterns similar to those during wakefulness, so this stage is also called paradoxical sleep: he is asleep with atonic limbs while his tracing looks awake. Rapid eye movement episodes recur about every 90 minutes. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 10, 9, 29, 28, 6"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman lies relaxed with her eyes closed at lights-out and then sleeps on into her deepest non-rapid eye movement stage. Which sequence of dominant waves is her electroencephalogram expected to show?","choices":["Alpha, then beta, then delta","Alpha, then theta, then delta","Beta, then alpha, then theta","Delta, then theta, then alpha"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed shows alpha waves; stages N1 and N2 show theta waves; and stage N3, the deepest non-rapid eye movement stage, shows delta waves. The frequency of the tracing becomes progressively slower as she passes from rest into deep sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 27, 29"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man settles into bed in a sleep laboratory, and a few minutes later he is drowsy and drifting off into light sleep. Which stage is he in?","choices":["Stage N3","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N1","Stage N2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage N1 is the transition from wakefulness: the person is somnolent or drowsy and in light sleep. Minutes after settling into bed and drifting off, he fits this first non-rapid eye movement stage, before the deeper stages N2 and N3. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 27, 6, 7"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man's electroencephalogram is compared between alert wakefulness in the evening and stage N3 later that night. Compared with his alert tracing, how does his stage N3 tracing differ?","choices":["Faster and higher in voltage","Slower and higher in voltage","Faster and lower in voltage","Slower and lower in voltage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Alert wakefulness shows beta waves: faster than 14 cycles per second, asynchronous and low voltage. Stage N3 shows delta waves: below 3.5 cycles per second, often with voltages 2 to 4 times greater than most other brain waves. The deep-sleep tracing is therefore slower and higher in voltage. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 23, 26, 28, 27"},{"q":"A 20-year-old student plans to sleep 3 hours a night for the 4 nights before an exam, reasoning that nobody knows what sleep is for anyway. What should he be told?","choices":["Sleep's role is unknown, so cutting it is safe","Harm needs months of short sleep","Short sleep mainly costs physical strength","Short nights can impair his thinking"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sleep restriction over a few days can decrease cognitive and physical performance, productivity and health, and the functions of sleep include cognition and the facilitation of learning or memory. Four nights of 3 hours is restriction over a few days, so his studying could backfire. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with epilepsy asks why his electroencephalogram looks different from a healthy person's. What determines the intensity and patterns of the electrical activity it records?","choices":["The activity of his peripheral nerves","His heart rate and blood pressure","How excited different brain regions are","Whether he is asleep or awake alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The intensity and patterns of electrical activity on an electroencephalogram are determined by the level of excitation of different parts of the brain, which changes with sleep, wakefulness and brain disorders such as epilepsy or psychosis. His epilepsy alters that excitation, and so his tracing. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 21, 26, 12"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man spends a full 8-hour night in a sleep laboratory. How many separate sleep cycles is his recording expected to show?","choices":["1 to 2 cycles","4 to 5 cycles","8 to 10 cycles","12 to 15 cycles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An average night is about 8 hours of sleep, and during those 8 hours there are 4 to 5 separate sleep cycles, each lasting 90 to 120 minutes. His full 8-hour recording should show 4 to 5 cycles, each closing with a rapid eye movement period. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 7, 9"},{"q":"A 29-year-old surgical resident has slept about 4 hours a night for the past 5 nights. Which effect of this sleep restriction is expected?","choices":["Lower cognitive and physical performance","Sharper focus from adapting to less sleep","Effects limited to daytime mood","No effect until weeks of restriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sleep restriction over a few days can cause decreased cognitive and physical performance, lower overall productivity and poorer health. Five nights of about 4 hours is restriction over a few days, so both his thinking and his physical work are expected to suffer. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman in a sleep study has moved into stage N2, the less responsive, deeper sleep that follows the drowsy transition. Which change in her body temperature and heart rate is expected?","choices":["They become irregular","Temperature rises, heart rate falls","They decrease","They increase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stage N2 is less responsive, deeper sleep in which heart rate and temperature decrease, fitting the slow heart rate and increased parasympathetic tone of non-rapid eye movement sleep. Stage N2 is also the longest stage by total duration. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 14"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has an overnight sleep recording. Compared with her first sleep cycle, what does her last cycle before morning show?","choices":["Deeper non-rapid eye movement sleep","No rapid eye movement period","A cycle under 30 minutes long","A longer rapid eye movement period"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Across a typical night, rapid eye movement periods grow longer in the later cycles, while the deepest non-rapid eye movement sleep falls in the first cycles. This fits rapid eye movement episodes lengthening as the sleeper becomes more rested. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 7, 9, 10, 6"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman is having a routine electroencephalogram. She is alert, chatting with the technician and looking around the room, and the tracing shows fast, low-voltage waves. The technician wants to record her alpha rhythm. What should she be asked to do?","choices":["Fall into deep sleep","Recall a frustrating event","Close her eyes and rest quietly","Solve sums in her head"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alpha waves appear when an adult is awake in a quiet, resting state, and closing the eyes in relaxation brings them out, most intensely over the occipital region. Her fast, low-voltage waves are beta waves from alert activity, which give way to alpha once she rests quietly with her eyes closed. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 24, 28, 23, 25, 26"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman sits relaxed with her eyes closed while her electroencephalogram shows alpha waves. She is then asked to multiply two numbers in her head. What change is expected on the tracing?","choices":["Alpha gives way to delta waves","Alpha waves grow larger","Alpha gives way to theta waves","Alpha gives way to beta waves"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When an awake person's attention is directed to a specific type of mental activity, alpha waves stop and are replaced by beta waves: faster than 14 cycles per second, asynchronous and low voltage, recorded mainly from the frontal and parietal regions during active processing. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 23, 22, 25, 26"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has an electroencephalogram while awake. She tells the technician she has felt deeply disappointed and frustrated since losing her job last week, and evaluation finds no brain disorder. The tracing shows waves at 4 to 7 cycles per second. Which explanation best fits these waves?","choices":["Alert mental activity","Her emotional stress","A normal resting adult rhythm","Very deep sleep"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Waves at 4 to 7 cycles per second are theta waves, which occur during emotional stress in some adults, especially during disappointment and frustration. With no brain disorder found and her disappointment and frustration since losing her job, emotional stress best explains them. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 25, 22, 26, 23"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man is waking up as his sleep centers stop being activated. Which structures are proposed to be released from inhibition and to activate spontaneously?","choices":["Spinal muscle control areas and motor neurons","Occipital and parietal regions of the cortex","Mesencephalic and upper pontile reticular nuclei","Sleep-promoting centers within the sleep centers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The proposed theory holds that when the sleep centers are not activated, the mesencephalic and upper pontile reticular activating nuclei are released from inhibition and activate spontaneously, exciting the cerebral cortex and peripheral nervous system, which feed back positively to keep them active. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 10, 28, 22"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man insists that he never dreams outside the dreaming stage because non-rapid eye movement sleep is dreamless. Which statement about non-rapid eye movement sleep is accurate?","choices":["Its dreams carry more muscle activity","Its dreams are the most vivid ones","Dreams occur but are often forgotten","It is truly a dreamless state"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-rapid eye movement sleep is often called a dreamless state, but that is not accurate: dreams and nightmares do occur. They are often not remembered because the dreams are not consolidated in memory, and they are less vivid than rapid eye movement dreams. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 13, 14, 9"},{"q":"A 34-year-old new mother has been sleeping about 4 hours a night for a week and has gone back to her office job. Which change is expected from this sleep restriction?","choices":["Better physical performance","No change for at least a month","Reduced productivity at work","More output from longer waking days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sleep restriction over a few days can decrease cognitive and physical performance, overall productivity and the health of a person. A week of about 4 hours a night is restriction over more than a few days, so lower productivity at work is expected. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman sits quietly with her eyes closed, relaxed but awake, during an electroencephalogram. The tracing shows rhythmical waves at 10 cycles per second, strongest over the occipital region. Which waveform is this?","choices":["Delta waves","Alpha waves","Beta waves","Theta waves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rhythmical waves at 8 to 13 cycles per second, most intense over the occipital region, are alpha waves. They are found in almost all healthy adults who are awake in a quiet, resting state, such as sitting relaxed with the eyes closed, and they disappear during deep sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 25, 26, 28"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man asks why trivial details of his day seem to fade after a night's sleep while what mattered stays. Which function of sleep explains this?","choices":["Neural maturation","Targeted erasure of synapses","Facilitating learning or memory","Clearing metabolic waste"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. One function of sleep is targeted erasure of synapses to forget unimportant information that might otherwise clutter the synaptic network. Losing trivial details while keeping what matters fits that function, which works alongside sleep's facilitation of learning or memory. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman's heart rate and blood pressure fall steadily after she enters non-rapid eye movement sleep. Which autonomic change explains this?","choices":["Increased sympathetic tone","Decreased parasympathetic tone","Inhibited spinal motor neurons","Increased parasympathetic tone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-rapid eye movement sleep brings increased parasympathetic tone, with low blood pressure, slow heart rate and decreased respiratory rate, together with decreased peripheral vascular tone. Her steady fall in heart rate and blood pressure is that parasympathetic shift. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 14, 12, 10, 28"},{"q":"A 4-month-old boy who is feeding well and developing normally has an electroencephalogram showing high-voltage waves below 3.5 cycles per second. Which explanation best fits this finding?","choices":["A normal finding in infancy","Serious organic brain disease","A comatose state","Emotional stress"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. High-voltage waves below 3.5 cycles per second are delta waves, and infancy is one of the settings where delta waves occur, along with very deep sleep and serious organic brain disease. In a healthy infant who is feeding well and developing normally, infancy itself explains them. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 5, 25"},{"q":"A healthy 8-year-old boy has an electroencephalogram while awake. Waves at 5 cycles per second appear over his parietal and temporal regions. Which waveform is this?","choices":["Theta waves","Alpha waves","Beta waves","Delta waves"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Theta waves have frequencies between 4 and 7 cycles per second and occur in the parietal and temporal regions in children. A 5-per-second rhythm over those regions in a healthy 8-year-old is theta, an expected finding at his age rather than a sign of disease. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 25, 22, 23, 26"}]};
